            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Turns a 2-D spectrum from np.fft.rfft2 back into an image: every wave across the grid is rebuilt at its strength and position and all of them are added up.\n\nRound trip it gives back the original exactly. The point is to change the spectrum in between: keep only the middle (the slow waves) to blur; remove the middle to keep only edges and detail; remove one bright point to take out a regular pattern - a mesh, a flicker, a scan line - while leaving everything else.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.fft.irfft2 <rows> <columns>\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nspectrum:\nThe complex spectrum, straight from np.fft.rfft2's spectrum outlet (not the centred magnitude). Every input is transformed and sent.\n\nshape (text box, also an inlet):\nThe size of the image to make: rows columns. Empty (the default) makes the last axis even - 2 x (columns - 1) - which is right for any image with an even number of columns; give the size for an odd one.\n\nnorm:\nAn option, under the node: must match the norm used for np.fft.rfft2.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nsignal:\nThe image. Always real.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n16 x 16 random values (left) go to their spectrum and straight back (right): the same picture, a new one every two seconds. Put any change to the spectrum between the two nodes to see what it does to the image.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.fft.rfft2 makes the spectrum. np.fft.irfft and np.fft.ifft are the 1-D inverses.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 900,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Turns a 2-D spectrum from np.fft.rfft2 back into an image: every wave across the grid is rebuilt at its strength and position and all of them are added up.\n\nRound trip it gives back the original exactly. The point is to change the spectrum in between: keep only the middle (the slow waves) to blur; remove the middle to keep only edges and detail; remove one bright point to take out a regular pattern - a mesh, a flicker, a scan line - while leaving everything else.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.fft.irfft2 <rows> <columns>\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nspectrum:\nThe complex spectrum, straight from np.fft.rfft2's spectrum outlet (not the centred magnitude). Every input is transformed and sent.\n\nshape (text box, also an inlet):\nThe size of the image to make: rows columns. Empty (the default) makes the last axis even - 2 x (columns - 1) - which is right for any image with an even number of columns; give the size for an odd one.\n\nnorm:\nAn option, under the node: must match the norm used for np.fft.rfft2.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nsignal:\nThe image. Always real.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n16 x 16 random values (left) go to their spectrum and straight back (right): the same picture, a new one every two seconds. Put any change to the spectrum between the two nodes to see what it does to the image.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.fft.rfft2 makes the spectrum. np.fft.irfft and np.fft.ifft are the 1-D inverses."
        },
